WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.781
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:05.081 --> 00:00:08.247
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:13.742
Unit three: Memorable Journeys. In this unit, you will tell detailed travel stories.

4
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:15.635
backpacking.

5
00:00:16.335 --> 00:00:19.705
backpacking means travelling cheaply with your things in a backpack.

6
00:00:20.005 --> 00:00:23.188
After graduating, she went backpacking around Vietnam.

7
00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:25.960
Your turn. Say: backpacking.

8
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.754
backpacking.

9
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.798
wanderlust.

10
00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:35.112
wanderlust means a strong desire to travel.

11
00:00:35.412 --> 00:00:38.352
My wanderlust began when I saw my first world map.

12
00:00:39.052 --> 00:00:41.832
Your turn. Say: wanderlust.

13
00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:45.580
wanderlust.

14
00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:47.573
stopover.

15
00:00:48.273 --> 00:00:51.666
stopover means a short stay in a place during a long journey.

16
00:00:51.966 --> 00:00:54.209
We had a two-day stopover in Singapore.

17
00:00:54.909 --> 00:00:57.074
Your turn. Say: stopover.

18
00:00:59.774 --> 00:01:00.856
stopover.

19
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.002
sightseeing trip.

20
00:01:03.702 --> 00:01:06.909
sightseeing trip means a trip to visit famous places.

21
00:01:07.209 --> 00:01:09.731
The sightseeing trip included five temples.

22
00:01:10.431 --> 00:01:13.072
Your turn. Say: sightseeing trip.

23
00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:17.156
sightseeing trip.

24
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.081
overland.

25
00:01:19.781 --> 00:01:23.743
overland means travelling by land, not by air or sea.

26
00:01:24.043 --> 00:01:26.751
They travelled overland from Phnom Penh to Bangkok.

27
00:01:27.451 --> 00:01:29.708
Your turn. Say: overland.

28
00:01:32.408 --> 00:01:33.444
overland.

29
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:35.718
once-in-a-lifetime.

30
00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:39.997
once-in-a-lifetime means very special and unlikely to happen again.

31
00:01:40.297 --> 00:01:43.759
Seeing the northern lights was a once-in-a-lifetime experience.

32
00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:46.984
Your turn. Say: once-in-a-lifetime.

33
00:01:49.684 --> 00:01:50.812
once-in-a-lifetime.

34
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:53.143
travel insurance.

35
00:01:53.843 --> 00:01:57.897
travel insurance means insurance that pays if something goes wrong on a trip.

36
00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:01.068
Never travel abroad without travel insurance.

37
00:02:01.768 --> 00:02:04.791
Your turn. Say: travel insurance.

38
00:02:07.491 --> 00:02:08.841
travel insurance.

39
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:10.770
off-season.

40
00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.292
off-season means the time of year when fewer tourists visit.

41
00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:17.859
Hotels are much cheaper in the off-season.

42
00:02:18.559 --> 00:02:20.723
Your turn. Say: off-season.

43
00:02:23.423 --> 00:02:24.401
off-season.

44
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:27.463
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:27.763 --> 00:02:29.739
Tell me about your most memorable journey.

46
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.803
Last year I went backpacking overland from Laos to Vietnam.

47
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:37.571
How long had you been planning it?

48
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:42.871
I'd been saving for two years. I'd been dreaming about it even longer!

49
00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:45.498
Did anything go wrong?

50
00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:50.687
I got ill during a stopover, but luckily I'd bought travel insurance.

51
00:02:51.983 --> 00:02:55.016
Grammar focus: Past perfect continuous.

52
00:02:55.316 --> 00:02:59.812
Use the past perfect continuous for an activity that continued up to a point in the past.

53
00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:01.275
Had been plus -ing.

54
00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:05.479
For example: I had been travelling for ten hours when the bus broke down.

55
00:03:05.779 --> 00:03:11.018
Use it to explain the cause of a past situation, or to show how long something had continued.

56
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:14.785
Now read and listen. The Slow Travel Movement.

57
00:03:15.085 --> 00:03:18.826
More and more travellers are choosing to travel overland instead of flying.

58
00:03:20.117 --> 00:03:24.566
Many of them had been taking short sightseeing trips for years before discovering slow travel.

59
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:29.939
They say that long train journeys let them meet local people and see real life.

60
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.119
Travelling in the off-season also reduces costs and crowds.

61
00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:40.529
For many, it turns an ordinary holiday into a once-in-a-lifetime experience.

62
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:44.330
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.943
A strong desire to travel is blank.

64
00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:50.606
A, wanderlust. B, stopover. C, off-season.

65
00:03:54.617 --> 00:03:56.988
The answer is A: wanderlust.

66
00:03:58.483 --> 00:04:00.854
I was exhausted because I blank all night.

67
00:04:01.154 --> 00:04:04.343
A, had been driving. B, have been driving. C, drove.

68
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.257
The answer is A: had been driving.

69
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.993
They blank for an hour when the rain started.

70
00:04:14.293 --> 00:04:17.378
A, had been hiking. B, have hiked. C, hike.

71
00:04:21.383 --> 00:04:23.557
The answer is A: had been hiking.

72
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.630
A short stay during a long journey is a blank.

73
00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.827
A, stopover. B, sightseeing trip. C, backpacking.

74
00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:37.607
The answer is A: stopover.

75
00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:42.707
According to the text, travelling in the off-season blank.

76
00:04:43.007 --> 00:04:48.692
A, is more expensive. B, reduces costs and crowds. C, is dangerous.

77
00:04:52.683 --> 00:04:55.774
The answer is B: reduces costs and crowds.

78
00:04:57.283 --> 00:05:00.969
Now it's your turn to speak. Describe a journey you will never forget.

79
00:05:06.269 --> 00:05:13.597
I'd been dreaming of Angkor for years, so when I finally saw the sunrise there, it felt like a once-in-a-lifetime moment.

80
00:05:13.897 --> 00:05:15.816
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:21.350
Great work today! You learned eight new words and past perfect continuous.

82
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.138
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

83
00:05:25.438 --> 00:05:27.707
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

84
00:05:28.007 --> 00:05:31.101
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

